  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Complex broad low pressure south of the area will \ntrack northward tonight into Sunday allowing rain to spread across \nthe area. Exact placement of rainfall still uncertain given the \ncomplexities of the low pressure and where the main low develops \nlater tonight or Sunday.\n\nLatest surface analysis and obs shows a broad area of low pressure \nacross northern FL and eastern GA along a frontal boundary that \nextends eastward into the Atlantic. There may be a second low \npressure area developing under convection over the Atlantic near \n30N/74W as satellite imagery show some curved bands over the \nsouthern semi-circle of the potential low. Meanwhile high \npressure is just off the NE coast. \n\nThe low over FL/GA is expected to continue to move northward today \ninto tonight and eventually become weaker as the low or wave to the \neast becomes the dominate low as it approaches the Outer Banks.\nHowever, the timing and location of this still remains highly \nuncertain and this will unfortunately be the main player in how \nmuch rainfall occurs at any one location (or if rain even falls \nas some guidance such as the 12z GFS suggest very little \nprecipitation over the area tomorrow). If the western low \ndissipates quickly and the coastal low takes over, inland areas \nmay not see as much rainfall as the inland rainfall will be \ndependent on the forcing from the western low. Meanwhile, if the\ncoastal low stays too far offshore, much of the area (even at \nthe coast) will see very little rainfall. Overall, the 1\" \nrainfall probabilities have decreased inland and as such have \nalso lowered precipitation amounts inland. We are still thinking\nthat rain will spread northward and into the area tomorrow as \nthe western low will likely maintain itself longer than what the\nGFS suggests. In addition, the PoPs across the southeast will \nremain lower as it looks like the best forcing from any coastal \nlow development will stay offshore and north of that area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mild on Monday, then much cooler through mid week. \nIncreasing potential for coastal low Wednesday or Thursday which may \nprove impactful especially at the coast. \n\nMonday will be mild behind the coastal low and before the stronger \nhigh pressure builds over the area. Widespread temperatures in the \nlow-mid 70s expected. Much cooler Tuesday and Wednesday with below \nnormal temps but dry. Then attention turns to the potential system \nfor around Thanksgiving.\n\nEnsemble consensus is in fair agreement in low pressure developing \nin the eastern Gulf of America or off the southeast coast and moving \nparallel to the coast by Thanksgiving. There are still placement and \ntiming disagreements which will originate Winchell from the \nsystems over the eastern Pacific this weekend and early next \nweek. However, there is an increasing likelihood of at least \nsome impacts at the coast simply due to the tight gradient \nbetween the expected 1040+mb high over New England and low \npressure to the south leading to prolonged and increasing NE \nwinds. In addition, this has the potential to bring better \nrainfall to the area with a decent tropical connection and \nstrong IVT nearby. Plus, NE winds especially near the coast \ncould be quite gusty due this pressure gradient and coastal \nflooding looks possible especially with the King Tide. This is \nstill 5 days out, and details will be better understood by early\nnext week as the system over the Pacific moves onshore over the\nwest coast.",
